.Dd August 16, 2026
.Dt GAFF 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm gaff
.Nd context-lifecycle handler for coding agents
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar command
.Op Ar options
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
manages session context for a coding agent through the harness hook
system: counters, cadence reminders, one-shot reminders, and prime
sections.
A hook exits 0 or 1, so a gaff fault never blocks a session.
Three things exit otherwise, on purpose: a guard refuses a tool call
with 2, a stop hold refuses a stop with 2, and
.Cm githook
returns the failing command's own code.
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width "remind <text>"
.It Cm hook
Handle one hook event from stdin.
The harness calls this; it is not for interactive use.
.It Cm remind Ar text Fl -after Ar n
Schedule a one-shot reminder that fires
.Ar n
tool calls ahead.
.Fl -after
is required.
.Fl -id Ar id
names the one-shot; scheduling the same id again re-arms it.
.Fl -session Ar id
selects the session; the default comes from the environment.
.It Cm status
Show the counters, the pending entries, and the one-shots.
.Fl -session Ar id
selects the session.
.It Cm init Oo Fl -host Ar name Oc Op Fl -uninstall
Register the agent hooks in the host's settings file.
Claude Code is the only implemented host, and it is the default.
.It Cm init Fl -git Op Fl -uninstall
Write one script in
.Pa .git/hooks
for each git entry the config declares.
gaff never sets
.Va core.hooksPath ,
because that setting names one directory and would disable every hook
another tool installed.
A hook gaff did not write is kept as
.Pa <hook>.local
and called first.
.It Cm init Fl -github
Render each declared workflow to
.Pa .github/workflows .
gaff cannot run a GitHub event, so this domain is generated and
checked, never executed.
.It Cm githook Ar name Op Ar args
Run one git hook.
The installed scripts call this.
It returns the failing command's exit code, because a git hook exists
to block the commit or the push.
.It Cm check Op Fl -handlers
Validate
.Pa .gaff/gaff.yml
and report every problem.
With
.Fl -handlers ,
validate
.Pa ~/.config/gaff/handlers.yml
instead.
With
.Fl -github ,
report a workflow that drifted from the config.
It exits 1 on a drift, so CI can run it.
The plain form stays repo-only, so it behaves the same in CI.
.It Cm doctor
Show what is live in this clone: config, hooks, and state.
.It Cm profile Oo Cm show | list | set Ar name Oc Op Fl -session Ar id
Show, list, or set the active profile.
A profile is a named overlay: it selects which reminders and sections
are active and may override their cadences.
The resolution path is the environment
.Ev GAFF_PROFILE ,
then the session state, then
.Pa .gaff/profile ,
then the config default.
A switch re-primes the sections on the next flush.
.Pp
Profiles are advisory.
gaff blocks nothing, and an agent that can write files can edit the
config regardless.
The transition policy refuses the agent-facing path: a profile absent
from
.Va transitions.agent_may_set
is human-only.
gaff decides who is asking by structural identity, where a terminal on
stdin is a human and anything else is an agent.
.It Cm trust
Allow handlers to run in this repo.
Every agent tool call passes through
.Cm gaff hook
first, and a built-in guard there refuses this command, so an agent
cannot grant consent through gaff.
The user's shell has no hook, so it runs there.
An agent that can write the home directory can still edit the file, so
this raises the cost and makes the grant visible; it is not a sandbox.
A handler's command runs with the repo as its working directory, and
tools such as
.Xr git 1 ,
.Xr make 1 ,
and just read executable settings from there.
So handlers are refused by default, and consent is per repo.
Consent is recorded in
.Pa ~/.config/gaff/trusted ,
outside every repo tree.
.It Cm log Op Fl -session Ar id
Print the injection audit trail: every flush gaff delivered to the
session, with the byte count and the entry names.
.It Cm allow Ar guard
Let the next tool call that
.Ar guard
would refuse through, once.
The built-in guard in
.Cm gaff hook
refuses this command from an agent; run it from your shell.
.It Cm ci Op Fl -hook Ar name
Run the declared git gates against HEAD, as CI does: the config must
load, no declared workflow may drift, then each hook's entries run,
pre-commit then pre-push, with a synthesized push on stdin for the
latter.
A requested hook with no entry fails the run.
.It Cm reviews Op Cm check Op Fl -head Ar sha
With no argument, print the reviews a change must pass, one to a line,
in declaration order.
A merge gate reads the list from here rather than from the config file,
so a format change cannot break the gate silently.
Where the repository config holds no
.Cm reviews
key, the command exits 1 and names the fix.
An absent policy must not read as "no review is required".
Where that config holds
.Ql reviews: [] ,
the command exits 0 and prints the user config's names, if it declares
any.
The user config and the repository config both contribute names, and
neither drops a name from the other.
A user config cannot state the policy for a repository that omits one.
.Pp
.Cm reviews check
reads git's pre-push ref lines on standard input and refuses a push
whose tips carry no sign-off for every declared review.
A sign-off is one line of the tip's
.Ql reviews
git note, reading
.Ql signoff[NAME] PASS SHA EVIDENCE .
Prose around the lines is ignored.
The sha names the commit the reviewer read, so a sign-off cannot carry
forward onto a commit nobody read.
Six faults refuse a push: a declared review with no sign-off, a verdict
of
.Ql FAIL ,
a sign-off naming another commit, two sign-offs for one review,
evidence under three words, and no note at all.
.Pp
.Fl -head Ar sha
names the reviewed commit for a pull-request event, whose checkout is a
merge commit no reviewer saw.
Only
.Cm ci
passes it.
It is an argument rather than an environment variable because a
pre-push hook takes its argv from the committed config, which a shell
cannot add to.
.It Cm docs Op Ar page
Print the bundled documentation.
Without
.Ar page ,
list the available pages.
.It Cm prime
Print what gaff is and how to use it, for an agent's context.
The output depends only on the binary version: no arguments, config,
or state changes it.
Put it into an agent's context; policy about when to use gaff belongs
to the caller.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width GAFF_STATE_DIR
.It Ev GAFF_STATE_DIR
The state root.
A relative path joins the working directory.
Without it, the root falls back to a user-scoped path.
The root is never inside the repo.
.It Ev GAFF_PROFILE
The active profile.
It outranks the session state and
.Pa .gaff/profile .
.It Ev GAFF_HANDLERS
Set it to
.Ql off
to disable every handler.
Reach for this when a handler wedges a session.
.It Ev GAFF_HOST
The host adapter for
.Cm gaff hook .
Without it, gaff selects the adapter from the payload shape.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "~/.config/gaff/handlers.yml"
.It Pa .gaff/gaff.yml
The repo config: reminders, sections, profiles, and cadences.
It never holds a command.
.It Pa ~/.config/gaff/gaff.yml
The same keys, for every repo.
The repo config lays over it, and a repo entry wins the name it
shadows.
The user value of
.Va transitions
wins, because a repo must not widen what an agent grants itself.
Both files contribute names to
.Va reviews ,
and neither drops a name from the other.
.It Pa ~/.config/gaff/handlers.yml
The handlers.
Only this file may declare a command.
.It Pa ~/.config/gaff/trusted
The repos that may run handlers.
.El
.Sh EVENTS
gaff names an event for what it means, not for what a host calls it.
An adapter maps its host's names onto this set:
.Bl -tag -width session_start -compact
.It Ic session_start
A session begins or resumes.
A flush point.
.It Ic prompt
The user submits a prompt.
A flush point.
.It Ic tool_call
One tool call finished.
gaff counts these.
.It Ic tool_batch
A batch of tool calls finished.
A flush point.
.It Ic stop
The agent finished a turn.
.El
.Pp
A host event outside this set stays first-class.
gaff forwards it and permits nothing.
.Sh HANDLERS
A handler is an external command whose stdout becomes injected context.
Handlers are declared only in
.Pa ~/.config/gaff/handlers.yml .
A repo cannot declare one, and gaff does not consult
.Ev GAFF_CONFIG_DIR
or
.Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
on the hook path, because a repo can set an environment variable
through direnv, mise, or a committed settings file.
.Pp
The first element of
.Va command
must be an absolute path.
gaff never searches
.Ev PATH ,
because a repo can prepend to it.
The child's environment is an allowlist, not a denylist, so it receives
no token unless a handler names one in
.Va env_passthrough .
Handlers run in sequence inside one deadline for each flush.
See
.Xr gaff-docs 1
or run
.Nm
.Cm docs configuration
for the full reference.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
An agent command exits 0 or 1, never 2.
Exit 2 is the agent host's blocking code, and no gaff failure may block
a session.
.Pp
.Cm githook
is the exception, and it is deliberate.
A git hook exists to block, so
.Cm githook
returns the exit code of the command that failed.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr missouri 1 ,
.Xr tisket 1
